          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            One fundamental tool in a knot theorist’s toolbox is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+diagram"} knot diagram
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot"} knot
            is an embedding of the circle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            into Euclidean space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            to obtain a 4-valent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+graph"} plane graph
            (referred to as the
            %em shadow
            of the knot), while keeping track of whether each vertex corresponds to an under-crossing or an over-crossing. Although the knot is traditionally seen as embedded in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 \times [0,1]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
            %em virtual knot theory
            generalizes classical knot theory by considering knots as embeddings of circles into thickened
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientable"} orientable
            surfaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times [0,1]
            of arbitrary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e., a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+map"} topological map
            %em not
            represent places where the knot passes over/under itself inside the thickened surface, but rather are artifacts of the knot’s non-planar shadow. So, in a
            %em virtual knot diagram
            such crossings are explicitly indicated as “virtual”, using a distinct notation from that for under/overcrossings.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p There are various equivalent definitions of virtual knots/links:
          %ul
            %li
              As equivalence classes of virtual knot/link diagrams, modulo the “virtual”
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reidemeister+moves"} Reidemeister moves
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Kauffman1999"} (Kauffman 1999)
            %li
              As equivalence classes of “abstract” knot/link diagrams embedded in orientable surfaces, modulo the Reidemeister moves
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#KamadaKamada"} (Kamada and Kamada 2000)
            %li
              As embeddings of circles into thickened orientable surfaces, considered up to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopy
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Kuperberg2003"} (Kuperberg 2003)
            %li
              As equivalence classes of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+codes"} Gauss codes
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Kauffman1999"} (Kauffman 1999)
          %h2#categorifications Categorifications
          %p
            The original work on virtual knot theory was not expressed in categorical language, but a first attempt at categorifying the
            %em virtual braid group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi VB
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} VB_n
            was made by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#KL2011"} Kauffman and Lambropoulou
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victoria+Lebed"} Victoria Lebed
            studied the question extensively in her thesis
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LebedThesis"} (Lebed 2012)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+object"} braided object
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            equipped with an invertible morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : V\otimes V \to V\otimes V
            satisfying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Baxter+equation"} Yang-Baxter equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\sigma \otimes V) \circ (V \otimes \sigma) \circ (\sigma \otimes V) = (V \otimes \sigma) \circ (\sigma \otimes V) \circ (V \otimes \sigma)
          %p
            In particular, Lebed shows that the virtual braid group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi VB
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} VB_n
            is isomorphic to the group of endomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi End
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mi br
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End_{\mathcal{C}_{2br}}(V^{\otimes n})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi br
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{2br}
            is the free symmetric monoidal category generated by a single braided object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            The intuition here is that the (symmetric) braiding of the ambient symmetric monoidal category represents virtual crossings “for free”, while the braiding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            on the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            represents “real” over- and under-crossings. (Compare some
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Baez08"} remarks by John Baez
          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %p
            Warning: a virtual knot/link has a genus in the sense of the genus of the underlying thickened surface into which it embeds (or equivalently, the genus of its shadow as a topological map), but this is unrelated to the classical notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em knot genus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seifert+surface"} Seifert surface
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
            is the knot.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+diagram"} Gauss diagram
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embedded+graph"} embedded graph
          %h2#references References
          %p The original paper on virtual knot theory and some early followup work:
          %ul
            %li#Kauffman1999
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Louis+Kauffman"}> Louis Kauffman
                , Virtual Knot Theory,
                %em European Journal of Combinatorics
                (1999) 20, 663-691.
                %a{:href => "http://homepages.math.uic.edu/~kauffman/VKT.pdf"} pdf
            %li#KamadaKamada
              %p
                Naoko Kamada and Seiichi Kamada, Abstract Link Diagrams and Virtual Knots,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Journal of Knot Theory and its Ramifications
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1142/S0218216500000049"} doi
            %li#Kuperberg2003
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Kuperberg"}> Greg Kuperberg
                , What is a virtual link?,
                %em Algebraic &amp; Geometric Topology
                Volume 3 (2003), 587-591.
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0208039v2.pdf"} pdf
          %p Miscellaneous papers:
          %ul
            %li#Kauffman2012
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Louis+Kauffman"}> Louis Kauffman
                , Introduction to Virtual Knot Theory. July 2012.
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.0665"} arXiv
            %li#Viro
              %p
                Oleg Viro, Virtual Links, Orientations of Chord Diagrams and Khovanov Homology, Proceedings of 12th Gökova Geometry-Topology Conference, pp. 184–209, 2005.
                %a{:href => "http://www.pdmi.ras.ru/~olegviro/ggt05-viro.pdf"} pdf
            %li#ManturovIlyutko
              %p
                Vassily Olegovich Manturov and Denis Petrovich Ilyutko,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Virtual Knots: The State of the Art
            %li#Kauffman2015
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Louis+Kauffman"}> Louis Kauffman
                , Rotational Virtual Knots and Quantum Link Invariants, 14 Oct 2015.
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1509.00578"} arxiv:1509.00578
          %p On the question of categorifying virtual knot theory, see:
          %ul
            %li#Baez08
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
                ,
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/10/categorification_in_new_scient.html#c019490"} comment at the n-Café
                following a post titled “Categorification in New Scientist”, October 2008.
            %li#KL2011
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Louis+Kauffman"} Louis Kauffman
                and Sofia Lambropoulou, A Categorical Structure for the Virtual Braid Group,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Comm. Algebra
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.ntua.gr/~sofia/publications/A22%20VirtualPureMia-my%20pdf.pdf"} pdf
            %li#LebedThesis
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victoria+Lebed"}> Victoria Lebed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Objets tressés: une étude unificatrice de structures algébriques et une catégorification des tresses virtuelles
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.maths.tcd.ie/~lebed/thesis_LEBED.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Lebed13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victoria+Lebed"}> Victoria Lebed
                , Categorical Aspects of Virtuality and Self-Distributivity,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Journal of Knot Theory and its Ramifications
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1142/S0218216513500454"} doi
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1206.3916"} arXiv:1206.3916
                is “an extended version of the above JKTR publication, containing in particular a chapter on free virtual shelves and quandles”.
